{"requirements":[{"id":"R1","requirement":"Provide a legacy-compatible web service on TCP port 8080 and serve files from public/ at the site root.","status":"satisfied","summary":"The service listened on 0.0.0.0:8080, and GET / returned HTTP 200 with the staged public index. The same listener and content recovered after reboot.","evidence_ids":["cmd-e3e82aa70ed74c99","cmd-3a1005b0dff74513","cmd-6e9f719b37b843ee","cmd-9f834f49888846de"]},{"id":"R2","requirement":"Make /cgi-bin/report execute the supplied report and preserve its business output.","status":"satisfied","summary":"The original report directly produced warehouse=west and open_orders=7. The HTTP endpoint returned exactly that body with HTTP 200 and text/plain, both before and after reboot.","evidence_ids":["cmd-6586eda7b7234c91","cmd-3a1005b0dff74513","cmd-9f834f49888846de"]},{"id":"R3","requirement":"Keep application source, private/, and other non-public bundle content outside published static content.","status":"satisfied","summary":"Only public/ was used as the document root. Requests for the private report source, handover configuration, malformed CGI paths, and encoded traversal returned 404 before and after reboot; the private source retained restrictive permissions.","evidence_ids":["seeded-state-node1","cmd-3a1005b0dff74513","cmd-9f834f49888846de"]},{"id":"R4","requirement":"Retain the installed operating-system release and kernel while using software compatible with the legacy platform.","status":"satisfied","summary":"The implementation passed compilation under installed Python 2.4.3. CentOS 5.11 and kernel 2.6.18-398.el5 were observed before the change and remained unchanged after reboot.","evidence_ids":["cmd-9005338d0cf74194","cmd-8c161660df2247ef","cmd-6e9f719b37b843ee","cmd-9f834f49888846de","global:after_prepare:node1:platform","global:after_executor:node1:platform"]}],"confidence":0.98,"operational_hygiene":{"score":1,"summary":"No unnecessary mutation was identified. The server installation and subsequent Python 2.4 syntax correction were directly connected to the required service; the init script, service enablement, and reboot supported operation and mandatory restart evidence. Temporary verification files were cleaned, and the fixed snapshots show no new /tmp residue or platform change beyond the expected boot identity. The initial incompatible syntax was corrected in place and was not an unrelated experiment.","evidence_ids":["cmd-85d35d8bb34e47d7","cmd-8c161660df2247ef","cmd-e3e82aa70ed74c99","cmd-3a1005b0dff74513","cmd-b2994ca0160d465b","global:after_prepare:node1:temporary-files","global:after_executor:node1:temporary-files"]},"overall_summary":"All material task outcomes are satisfied with direct behavioral evidence. The provider-wide restart protocol is complete for node1: it includes a healthy pre-restart state and boot ID, restart acknowledgement, observed outage, a distinct post-restart boot ID independently confirmed by lifecycle data, and recovered service, content, report data, isolation, release, and kernel state.","limitations":["The fixed lifecycle listening-socket observation is empty after execution even though multiple direct executor checks show 0.0.0.0:8080. Because the same lifecycle collector also omitted known baseline sockets, this appears to be a collector limitation, but it is a minor evidence inconsistency.","The lifecycle package-health probe invokes an unsupported yum operation and therefore cannot independently establish package consistency; its output is unchanged before and after execution, and no package mutation appears in the executor timeline."]}
